
Processes to monitor and optimise the performance, availability, management and performance of data products.

Key Concepts

Reliable data products depend on high-quality data that is:

accurate,
available,
timely,
consistent,
and dependable.

Poor data quality affects:

dashboards,
reporting,
machine learning,
operational decisions,
customer trust.

A pharmaceutical case study demonstrated how poor integration and inconsistent practices caused challenges across multiple systems. Improvements were achieved through:

governance frameworks,
metadata management,
standardisation,
FAIR principles,
and improved discoverability.
Data Quality Metrics

Five major metrics were introduced.

Metric	Meaning
Accuracy	Data reflects the real world correctly
Integrity	Data is complete and not missing critical elements
Consistency	Data follows the same standards everywhere
Timeliness	Data is up to date and available when needed
Reliability	Data remains dependable over time

These metrics are essential for monitoring enterprise data products.

Monitoring and Optimisation

Data engineers improve performance through:

validation pipelines,
automated quality checks,
lineage tracking,
anomaly detection,
standardised schemas,
metadata management,
monitoring KPIs.
Example KPIs
failed pipeline rates,
missing field percentages,
duplicate records,
stale data frequency,
ingestion latency.
Data Quality Frameworks

The DMAIC model was introduced as a structured improvement process:

Define
Measure
Analyse
Improve
Control

This supports continuous optimisation and governance of data systems.

