K5 — Risks, Ethics and Data Quality

KSB:
The inherent risks of data such as incomplete data, ethical data sources and how to ensure data quality.

Common Data Quality Risks

The session identified several common issues:

missing records,
missing attributes,
duplicate elements,
outdated values,
incorrect values,
illogical event ordering,
non-standard formats.

These issues can severely affect:

analytics accuracy,
business decisions,
compliance,
automation systems.
Data Quality Metrics and Risk
Accuracy

Incorrect data creates false conclusions.

Integrity

Missing information reduces completeness.

Consistency

Different formats create conflicts across systems.

Timeliness

Old data creates unreliable reporting.

Reliability

Unstable data cannot support strategic decisions.

Ethical and Governance Considerations

Data stewardship was introduced as a critical governance function.

Responsibilities include:

maintaining quality,
ensuring compliance,
protecting integrity,
enabling accountability.
Data Stewardship Best Practices
data ownership,
validation processes,
feedback loops,
cleansing initiatives,
reporting improvements,
governance standards.
FAIR Data Principles

Four FAIR principles were discussed:

Principle	Meaning
Findable	Data can be discovered easily
Accessible	Authorised users can retrieve it
Interoperable	Systems can exchange and interpret it
Reusable	Data can be used repeatedly

These principles improve:

governance,
integration,
collaboration,
long-term usability.
Sustainability and Ethical Practice

Sustainable data engineering also contributes to responsible data management.

Examples included:

reducing duplication,
lifecycle management,
rationalised storage,
efficient transfer protocols.

These reduce:

energy consumption,
storage waste,
environmental impact.
K18 — Streaming, Batch and On-Demand Data Movement

KSB:
How to use streaming, batching and on-demand services to move data from one location to another.

Key Concepts

Data movement between systems introduces quality risks if:

formats differ,
schemas conflict,
encoding varies,
validation is missing.

Three major formats were examined:

XML,
CSV,
JSON.
XML Quality Challenges
Common Problems
parsing errors,
encoding inconsistencies,
schema violations.
Mitigation Techniques
schema validation,
standardised encoding,
sanitisation,
structured parsers,
testing.

XML is common in:

enterprise systems,
APIs,
configuration management.
CSV Quality Challenges
Common Problems
inconsistent delimiters,
missing headers,
data type ambiguity,
malformed records.
Mitigation Techniques
robust parsers,
explicit schemas,
validation rules,
standardised formatting.

CSV remains heavily used in:

payroll exports,
operational reporting,
data migration.
JSON Quality Challenges
Common Problems
deeply nested structures,
inconsistent key naming,
large file sizes,
missing fields.
Mitigation Techniques
naming conventions,
validation rules,
streaming,
pagination,
compression.

JSON is widely used in:

APIs,
NoSQL systems,
web applications,
event-driven systems.
Practical Data Transfer Considerations

Data engineers should:

validate incoming records,
standardise timestamps,
support Unicode,
manage schema evolution,
optimise ingestion pipelines.
K27 — Descriptive, Predictive and Prescriptive Analytics

KSB:
The principles of descriptive, predictive and prescriptive analytics.

Relationship Between Data Quality and Analytics

Analytics quality depends entirely on data quality.

Poor data causes:

inaccurate dashboards,
biased models,
unreliable predictions,
misleading recommendations.
Descriptive Analytics

Uses historical data to explain:

“What happened?”

Examples:

HR dashboards,
operational reports,
turnover summaries.

Requires:

accurate aggregation,
standardised formats,
reliable timestamps.
Predictive Analytics

Uses models to estimate:

“What may happen?”

Examples:

employee churn prediction,
forecasting,
anomaly detection.

Requires:

clean training data,
consistency,
validated features.
Prescriptive Analytics

Uses predictions to guide:

“What should we do?”

Examples:

staffing interventions,
operational optimisation,
targeted engagement strategies.

High-quality data is essential because poor recommendations can:

create unfair outcomes,
increase business risk,
reduce trust in AI systems.
